st=storypage('nishumbha','NIŚUMBHA FALLS','Ślokas 9.1–41',
 panels('Three panels: Nishumbha’s weapons cut one by one; Shumbha in his eight-armed chariot; a man stepping out of Nishumbha’s pierced heart',[
  ('#ffe3c4','WEAPON BY WEAPON',ASURA(50,170,.8,'#5a2a6a')+SWORD(90,120,30,.7)+TRIDENT(110,110,.5)+DEVI(110,10,.35,False)),
  ('#2b2140','EIGHT ARMS',CHARIOT(72,170,.8)+ASURA(72,130,.7,'#3a5a6a')+''.join(f'<path d="M72 100 l{40*math.cos(math.pi+k*0.45):.0f} {30*math.sin(math.pi+k*0.45):.0f}" stroke="#7a9a5a" stroke-width="3"/>' for k in range(8))),
  ('#fde4e4','“STOP!”',ASURA(60,170,.8,'#5a2a6a')+ASURA(90,130,.45,'#7a3a7a')+DEVI(120,0,.35,False))]),
 ['<b>The king asks.</b> “Tell me what Śumbha and the furious Niśumbha did next.”',
  '<b>Niśumbha’s weapons.</b> He strikes her lion; she cuts his sword, shield, spear, trident, mace and axe, and fells him with arrows.',
  '<b>Śumbha rises.</b> Eight-armed in his chariot, he fills the sky. Conch, bow, bell, lion and Kālī answer; she cries “Stop!” and the gods cry “Victory!” Her trident drops him in a faint.',
  '<b>Niśumbha again.</b> With ten thousand arms and discuses; she cuts them all, pierces his heart, and beheads the man who steps out of it.',
  '<b>The army.</b> The lion, Kālī, Śivadūtī and the Mothers finish the rest. Some flee. Only Śumbha remains.'],
 ("Why does a man step out of his heart?","The Purāṇa doesn't explain. Many read it as the inner demon, the one hiding behind the outer one. Notice: she doesn't fight it. She laughs and finishes it in one stroke."),
 'Pierce the heart; <b>the hidden self steps out, and falls</b>.')
P=dict(n=14,ch=9,out='/home/claude/work/devim/Devi_Mahatmya_Part14.html',
 title='Niśumbha · Part 14 · Devī Māhātmya series · srini.presents',
 desc='Devī Māhātmya, Part 14: chapter 9. Niśumbha and Śumbha fight the Goddess themselves; Śumbha rises eight-armed, Niśumbha ten-thousand-armed; she pierces Niśumbha’s heart and beheads the man who steps out of it. Explained in plain English with illustrations.',
 h1='NIŚUMBHA',series='DEVĪ MĀHĀTMYA · PART 14 · CHAPTER 9',tag='The younger brother falls · explained and illustrated',cover=cover,
 how='The story in <b>comic panels</b>, and the key ślokas of chapter 9 on their own pages: the chant, a picture, what it says, the key words, one insight and the one thing to remember.',
 where=[f'In {plink(13)}, the Seven Mothers came out of the gods, and Cāmuṇḍā drank the blood of Raktabīja until he fell, bloodless.',
  'Chapter 9 opens with King Suratha asking for more. Then the two tyrant brothers ride out themselves. This chapter belongs to the younger, <b>Niśumbha</b>.'],
 wtalk=("Two brothers. Why two?","Some teachers read Śumbha and Niśumbha as ‘I’ and ‘mine’, ahaṃkāra and mamakāra. Mine falls first; I falls last. It's a reading, not the text, but it fits the story well."),
 fulls=[('THE KEY VERSES IN ONE GO','Chapter 9, ślokas 2–22',FV[:15]),('IN ONE GO · CONTINUED','Ślokas 24–41',FV[15:])],
 fullnote='Text as given in the Devīmāhātmyam edition of devimahatmyam.aurobharati.in (41 ślokas in chapter 9); its numbering is followed.',
 seq=[st]+V,
 toc=[('THE STORY',[('nishumbha','Niśumbha Falls')]),('THE KEY VERSES',V)],
 end=['<b>The frame (9.1–3).</b> King Suratha, absorbed now, asks what happened next.',
  '<b>Niśumbha (9.4–16).</b> He strikes her lion; she cuts every weapon he lifts and fells him.',
  '<b>Śumbha (9.17–28).</b> Eight-armed in his chariot; conch, bow, bell, lion and Kālī answer; “Stop!” “Victory!”; her trident drops him in a faint.',
  '<b>The heart (9.29–36).</b> Niśumbha rises with ten thousand arms; she pierces his heart, laughs at the man who steps out, and beheads him.',
  '<b>The army (9.37–41).</b> The lion, Kālī, Śivadūtī and the Mothers finish it. Only Śumbha remains.'],
 endsub='Chapter 9 in five lines',
 next=("What's next?",'Part 15: chapter 10. Śumbha taunts her: “You fight with the help of others.” She answers: “I am alone in this world. Who else is there besides me?” And all the Mothers merge into her.'),
 endkey='<b>pierce the heart of pride, and its hidden self falls</b>.',
 foot='Study notes on the Devī Māhātmya, chapter 9 (Mārkaṇḍeya Purāṇa). The story is retold in our words; ślokas are given as in the Devīmāhātmyam edition of devimahatmyam.aurobharati.in, whose numbering is followed. The readings of the brothers as “I and mine” and of the man from the heart are interpretive. Meanings are our own plain-English renderings. Zappy and Prof. Bolt are fictional.')
